NAME
    rsudo - execute a command under sudo on a remote host

SYNOPSIS
    rsudo [--interactive] [--askpass] [--ssh-auth-check]
          [--connect user@host] [--load file:group] [--user sudo_as_user]
          [--no-preserve-quotes] [submodule] [--] [args...]

DESCRIPTION
    rsudo connects to a remote host through SSH and executes the requested target
    under remote sudo. It is the command entrypoint for the rsudo library.

    Connection and credential state may be supplied through RSUDO_HOST,
    RSUDO_USER and RSUDO_PASSWORD, through --connect and --load, or through the
    documented password-acquisition paths.

    Connection/credential state, sudo target-user state and interactive mode may
    remain active in the current shell and are inherited by recursive rsudo
    calls. --user and --interactive update those reusable values.

    Askpass, no-preserve-quotes and ssh-auth-check are invocation-local: every
    rsudo call clears those modes before parsing its own options.

    A literal -- ends rsudo option and submodule interpretation and sends the
    remaining operands to normal remote execution.

OPTIONS
    --ssh-auth-check
        Perform an interactive SSH preparation followed by a real ssh_auth
        verification, then return without executing sudo or a remote target.

        The check requires a TTY and accepts no remaining command/submodule
        operands. The first SSH connection uses StrictHostKeyChecking=ask,
        AddKeysToAgent=yes and ControlPath=none so host enrollment and ordinary
        OpenSSH credential interaction can occur. The second connection uses
        ssh_auth with RSUDO_PASSWORD and ControlPath=none to verify the normal
        rsudo authentication path.

    --interactive
        Force interactive remote execution through a terminal-capable path.

    --askpass
        When standard input is not a TTY, consume the first input record as the
        rsudo password before forwarding the remaining input to the operation.

    --connect user@host
        Set the remote login user and host. The user may be empty and then falls
        back to USER; the host must be non-empty.

    --load file:group
        Split the operand at its final ':'. file and group are independent and
        either may be empty.

        file:group
            Load/evaluate file, then select group credentials.

        file:
            Load/evaluate file without selecting or replacing credentials from
            a group.

        :group
            Select group credentials already present in shell memory without
            loading a file.

        :
            Load no file and select no group; preserve current connection state.

        A non-empty group selects:
            RSUDO_CREDENTIALS_GROUP_<group>_HOST
            RSUDO_CREDENTIALS_GROUP_<group>_USER
            RSUDO_CREDENTIALS_GROUP_<group>_PASS

        The ':' separator is mandatory.

    --user sudo_as_user
        Execute the remote target as sudo_as_user, subject to remote sudo policy.

    --no-preserve-quotes
        Disable normal rsudo command normalization for this invocation.

    --
        End rsudo option/submodule processing.

    The current rsudo option surface uses the long option spellings above. The
    former short aliases -n, -i and -A are not rsudo options.

RECURSIVE CALLS
    Submodules execute in the current shell and may call rsudo again.

    Recursive rsudo calls reuse current connection/credential state such as
    RSUDO_HOST, RSUDO_USER and RSUDO_PASSWORD. They also inherit current
    RSUDO_AS_USER and RSUDO_INTERACTIVE state.

    RSUDO_ASKPASS, RSUDO_NO_PRESERVE_QUOTES and RSUDO_SSH_AUTH_CHECK are
    cleared at each rsudo call and therefore are not inherited implicitly.

ENVIRONMENT
    RSUDO_HOST
        Remote SSH host.

    RSUDO_USER
        Remote SSH login user. USER is used when RSUDO_USER is empty and USER is
        available.

    RSUDO_PASSWORD
        Non-empty authentication value used by normal rsudo as the ssh_auth
        candidate secret and made available to remote sudo when needed.

    RSUDO_CREDENTIALS_GROUP_<group>_HOST
    RSUDO_CREDENTIALS_GROUP_<group>_USER
    RSUDO_CREDENTIALS_GROUP_<group>_PASS
        Named credentials selectable through --load.

    USER
        Fallback login user when RSUDO_USER is empty.

    RSUDO_AS_USER and RSUDO_INTERACTIVE are reusable rsudo caller state and may
    be inherited by recursive calls. RSUDO_ASKPASS,
    RSUDO_NO_PRESERVE_QUOTES and RSUDO_SSH_AUTH_CHECK are invocation-local and
    are cleared before each rsudo invocation parses its options.

EXEC INJECT SUBMODULE
    rsudo exec inject
        [library-reference | --command command-name local-source]...
        [-- command-source [command-arg...]]

    The exec inject operation generates an in-memory library/source program
    through loadlib_inject_stream and pipes that complete generated source into a
    recursive rsudo call.

    Selected libraries and repeatable --command registrations are explicit and
    follow the loadlib_inject_stream contract. Each --command associates a
    caller-selected shell function identifier with one local source file and
    executes that source in a subshell whenever invoked.

    An optional one-shot command-source and its arguments follow --. The one-shot
    source also executes through an isolated generated subshell wrapper.

    When input remains after outer password acquisition, non-TTY standard input
    is appended by loadlib_inject_stream as shell source after named-command
    definitions and the optional one-shot command invocation. It is therefore
    source code, not runtime stdin for an injected command.

    Because injected command bodies execute in subshells, their exit, exec,
    positional-parameter, trap, cwd, umask, file-descriptor and shell-option
    effects do not terminate or mutate the containing continuation shell.
    Registered named commands remain callable by the one-shot command and by
    subsequent input source.

    The recursive rsudo call inherits current target-user and interactive state.
    Source generation completes before recursive execution begins. A generator
    failure is returned without invoking recursive rsudo; after successful
    generation the recursive rsudo status is propagated.

INPUT AND OUTPUT
    Non-interactive execution preserves the target's intended standard input.
    Authentication data is not delivered to the target as ordinary input.

    Target stdout and stderr remain observable to the caller.

    Interactive execution uses a remote terminal path suitable for TTY-requiring
    commands.

    With --interactive and non-TTY standard input, the remaining input after any
    --askpass password record is consumed locally as shell source for the remote
    privileged operation. A non-empty source stream is executed under remote sudo
    in the same interactive shell environment as the requested command.

    When command operands are present, their invocation follows the injected
    source. Normal mode preserves their argument meaning; --no-preserve-quotes
    applies its alternate command-passing behavior to that appended invocation.

    With no command operands, a non-empty source stream is the complete remote
    shell program. The source stream is not forwarded as ordinary target stdin.

EXIT STATUS
    When remote execution is reached, rsudo propagates the resulting operation
    status.

    Local invocation/setup failures use:
        1   missing --user operand
        2   missing --connect operand
        3   --connect contains more than one @
        4   --connect contains no @
        5   --connect has an empty host
        6   missing --load operand
        7   --load operand contains no :
        8   unknown long rsudo option
        9   empty RSUDO_HOST after option/load processing
        10  no usable RSUDO_USER and no USER fallback
        11  --askpass password read from stdin failed
        12  password read from TTY failed
        13  RSUDO_PASSWORD is empty after acquisition
        14  invalid delegated submodule function name
        15  rsudo submodule load failed
        16  delegated submodule function is unavailable
        253 --ssh-auth-check received unexpected remaining operands
        254 --ssh-auth-check requires a TTY
        255 --ssh-auth-check authentication verification failed

SECURITY
    RSUDO_PASSWORD is authentication data. It must not become ordinary target
    input/output or be exposed merely because a remote authentication step does
    not consume it.

